\DOC GEN_X86_ADD_RETURN_STACK_TAC

\TYPE {GEN_X86_ADD_RETURN_STACK_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> thm -> term -> int -> int * int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a top-level {ensures} correctness goal for a subroutine wrapped in a
stack-frame/register-save prologue and epilogue, deriving it from a supplied
core correctness theorem, either closing the goal or leaving one residual goal
about the resulting state.

\DESCRIBE
{execth : thm * (thm option array)} supplies the executable-decoding data for
the machine code: a code-length theorem together with the per-instruction
decode array. {coreth : thm} is the correctness theorem for the core of the
routine, which the tactic specialises and composes with the wrapper.
{reglist : term} is a HOL list of the registers the wrapper saves and restores,
{stackoff : int} the size of the stack frame subtracted from the incoming stack
pointer, and {(n,m) : int * int} the instruction counts of the wrapper's
prologue and epilogue. It applies to an assumption-free goal that is
universally quantified (or existentially-then-universally quantified) and whose
conclusion is an {ensures} statement for the full wrapped routine. Either the
goal is closed outright, or a single goal remains in which all quantifiers
{x1 ... xn} have been stripped, the hypotheses of the implication appear as
assumptions in expanded ({nonoverlapping_modulo}) form, many further derived
facts about the final state are added as assumptions — loaded code, restored
register values, flag values, saved stack slots, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation between initial and final states, final instruction pointer and stack
pointer, and the event trace — and the conclusion is reduced to the remaining
postcondition component for that state:
{
   A ?- t
   =============================  GEN_X86_ADD_RETURN_STACK_TAC execth coreth reglist stackoff (n,m)
              (no goals)
      or
   A, hyps, state facts ?- u
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only these two outcomes occur, on goals that initially
have no assumptions.

\ENDDOC
